# CSdump - Capstone Disassembler Engine

This directory contains csdump of Capstone Engine.

Csdump is a command-line tool to disassemble binary files.
For example, to decode a binary file for Intel 64bit, run:

```bash
$ csdump -a x86 -m 64 -i program.bin
0x0:	48 89 e5     	mov	rbp, rsp
0x3:	48 83 ec 10  	sub	rsp, 0x10
0x7:	c7 45 fc 00 00 00 00	mov	dword ptr [rbp - 4], 0
0xe:	8b 45 fc     	mov	eax, dword ptr [rbp - 4]
```

Csdump disassembles the input binary file and prints out the assembly instructions.
On each line, the first column is the instruction address, the second
column is opcodes, and the rest is the instruction itself.

Csdump is flexible enough to handle various binary formats. The tool automatically
detects ELF, PE/MZ, and raw binary formats.

```bash
$ csdump -a arm -m thumb -i firmware.bin
$ csdump -a riscv -m 64 -i riscv_binary  
$ csdump -a mips -m 32 -i mips_program
```

To print out instruction details, run Csdump with -d option, like below.

```bash
$ csdump -d -a x86 -m 32 -i sample.bin
0x1000:	01 d8        	add	eax, ebx
    Groups: 0 
    Implicit registers read: 
    Implicit registers written: 17 
    
0x1002:	89 ec        	mov	esp, ebp
    Groups: 0 
    Implicit registers read: 21 
    Implicit registers written: 39 
```

To see all the supported options, run ./csdump